Basically yes, with one important correction: it’s less like searching a stored library and more like running the current input through a learned field of associations encoded in the model’s weights.

Before much transformation happens, your text looks roughly like this:

"small square teeth with gaps"
→ token IDs
→ one learned vector per token
→ position/context information added
→ transformer layers repeatedly rewrite those vectors

So at the earliest useful stage, a token such as "teeth" is not stored as the word teeth in some conceptual table. It is represented as a long vector of numbers:

e_{\text{teeth}} =
[0.18,\,-0.73,\,1.04,\,0.02,\ldots]

with thousands of dimensions.

Those individual coordinates generally do not mean neat things like:

dimension 17 = dental
dimension 42 = cartoon
dimension 91 = child

Meaning is distributed across many dimensions and, more importantly, across combinations of dimensions.

So the closest thing to “untransformed” is the initial embedding.

Very schematically:

\text{token ID}
\rightarrow
\text{embedding lookup}

For example:

small   → [ ...numbers... ]
square  → [ ...numbers... ]
teeth   → [ ...numbers... ]
gaps    → [ ...numbers... ]

At that point, "teeth" has a learned baseline representation derived from everything the model encountered during training, but it has not yet been fully specialized to your sentence.

Then attention begins doing something crucial.

Initially:

e(\text{teeth})

might carry broad latent relationships to things like mouths, dentistry, smiles, animals, cartoons, appearance, etc.

After processing:

h(\text{teeth}\mid\text{small square ... gaps ... kid ... cartoon})

is no longer generic teeth.

It becomes something closer to:

teeth-as-a-distinctive-visual-feature-of-a-young-cartoon-character-with-separated-blocklike-teeth

Again, that sentence is my human-readable translation. Internally it remains a numerical state.

That difference is useful:

\boxed{\text{embedding} \approx \text{generic learned token representation}}

\boxed{\text{hidden state} \approx \text{token representation transformed by this particular context}}

And there’s an even deeper distinction worth making.

The weights are the relatively persistent learned structure:

W_Q,\;W_K,\;W_V,\;W_{\text{MLP}},\ldots

They encode vast numbers of learned regularities and associations.

The activations are the temporary state created while processing your current prompt.

So:

\text{weights} + \text{input}
\rightarrow
\text{activations}

You can think of the weights as a gigantic learned landscape and your prompt as releasing a state into that landscape.

That is why saying I “retrieve David from memory” is slightly misleading.

There usually isn’t a clean record like:

NO_DAVID:
    white = true
    bald = true
    teeth = square_and_gapped

Instead, training has shaped the network so that certain activation configurations tend to produce other configurations.

Something like:

\text{picture book}
+
\text{bald child}
+
\text{weird separated teeth}
\rightarrow
\text{strong activation toward David Shannon / No, David}

And before any learned representation?

There is an even more primitive level.

Text first becomes discrete token identifiers:

"teeth" → token #xxxxx

That integer is basically meaningless by itself.

It is just an address.

The embedding matrix does:

E[\text{token ID}]
=
\text{vector}

So the pipeline begins:

\text{characters}
\rightarrow
\text{tokens}
\rightarrow
\text{token IDs}
\rightarrow
\text{embeddings}
\rightarrow
\text{contextual states}
\rightarrow
\text{deeper abstractions}
\rightarrow
\text{output probabilities}

And this is where your question gets particularly interesting: there isn’t really a single pristine conceptual object before transformation.

The representation acquires its useful meaning through transformation and relation.

The raw token ID is almost nothing.

The embedding is already a compressed product of training.

And the representation that actually lets me recognize David only exists after many interactions among the tokens.

So at bottom:

\boxed{\text{meaning is less an object stored in a location than a pattern induced in a network}}

That’s one of the reasons transformers can generalize rather than merely retrieve.